+
+### Decision 6 — The abandoned-upload reaper (designed here, implemented in #625)
+
+**(a) The protocol-facing half.**
+
+- **The liveness observable, derived only from records the protocol durably writes.** No
+  settled design carries a per-part session counter, and none is assumed here. A session's
+  *last progress instant* is
+  `max(mpu.created_at_millis, max over its part records of committed_at_millis)`, and a
+  session additionally counts as live while **any session-owned `pending:` entry holds an
+  unexpired lease** — which is exactly what a single long `UploadPart` produces, because the
+  streaming write renews its leases every half-TTL while the call is in flight
+  (`crates/core/src/write.rs:474-500`). A 5 GiB part streaming for hours is therefore
+  *observably* progressing without inventing a new record or a new heartbeat.
+- **Abandonment rule.** A session is abandoned when *both* hold: (i) its last progress instant
+  is older than `W_open`, and (ii) it owns no unexpired `pending:` lease. `Completing` sessions
+  use the separate, shorter `W_completing` and are **rolled back** (`Completing → Open`), never
+  reaped directly — rollback is always safe because the epoch bump invalidates any publication
+  batch the crashed completer might still land.
+- **What the reaper may exit** — every state in §2's exit table: abandoned `Open` (fence to
+  `Aborting`), stale `Completing` (roll back to `Open`), any `retire:` obligation (drain), any
+  session-owned `pending:` entry whose session is gone or fenced (decision 5), and finally the
+  session record itself. **Once the reaper exists, no protocol state is unexitable** — this is
+  what closes F1 globally rather than case by case.
+- **The clock lifecycle and its owner (F10).** The abandonment judgment reads exactly one
+  clock lifecycle: the **deployment wall clock**, stamped by the gateway into
+  `created_at_millis` / `committed_at_millis` and evaluated by the reaper — the same
+  cross-component lifecycle the overwrite path already uses, where `orphaned_at_millis` is the
+  commit's own instant stamped by the gateway (`crates/core/src/write.rs:305-313`) and
+  evaluated by the custodian against the grace window (`gc.rs:171-176`). It is
+  `SystemTime::now()`, which madsim virtualises, so DST determinism holds
+  ([ADR-0009][a9]; the [ADR-0047][a47] publication timestamp is the worked precedent).
+  The session record records its `clock_source`, and **the reaper skips (and alarms on) any
+  session whose `clock_source` it does not own** — fail-safe, no reap. No logical-clock
+  producer may open a session; the CLI, the one such producer in tree
+  (`crates/server/src/cli.rs:629-637`, the fixed logical `NOW_MILLIS`), has no multipart verb,
+  and the tag is what keeps that true by evidence rather than by assumption.
+- **Admission control — the enforceable bound (F7).** `CreateMultipartUpload` refuses (S3
+  `503 SlowDown`) when the open-session population is at `MAX_OPEN_SESSIONS`. The population is
+  read by one `scan("mpu:")`, itself bounded by that same cap by induction, so admission needs
+  no hot counter and no coordination; two concurrent creates can overshoot by at most the
+  number of creates in flight fleet-wide, which the cap's headroom absorbs. The bounding
+  invariant is
+  `(MAX_OPEN_SESSIONS + overshoot) × MAX_PARTS_PER_SESSION + headroom ≤ SCAN_CAP`, with
+  `MAX_PARTS_PER_SESSION = 10_000` (S3's own limit). **Admission is what bounds the namespace
+  when the reaper is unavailable; the reaper is what makes the system make progress.** An
+  asynchronous collector alone establishes no cardinality bound, which is precisely why both
+  are required and why neither alone is offered as F7's disposal.
+
+**(b) The algorithm.**
+
+```text
+one reaper pass (dispatched from the fenced control point, like every custodian loop):
+  1. sessions = scan("mpu:")                      # bounded by MAX_OPEN_SESSIONS
+  2. for each session, in id order:               # deterministic order, resumable
+       progress = max(created_at, max part committed_at)   # scan("part:<id>:") — <= 10_000
+       if clock_source not ours:      alarm; skip
+       if state == Open       and now - progress > W_open       and no live owned lease:
+             CAS Open@E -> Aborting@E+1 + put retire:bytes:{session, all}  # one O(1) batch
+       if state == Completing and now - progress > W_completing:
+             CAS Completing@E -> Open@E+1                                  # rollback, safe
+       if state == Completed  and now - completed_at > W_tombstone and no obligation left:
+             delete mpu:<id>
+  3. obligations = scan("retire:")                # bounded by the same admission argument
+  4. for each obligation: drain at most B mutations, then commit; repeat next pass
+       retire:bytes:   orphan-mark this batch of fragments  (idempotent; never re-stamp)
+                       -> when all marked: delete the part records the payload names
+                       -> when all deleted: delete the obligation; delete the session
+                          record once it has no obligation left and its window elapsed
+       retire:records: delete this batch of the part records the payload names
+                       -> when all deleted: delete the obligation (never orphan-mark)
+  5. owned = scan("pending:") filtered to entries whose owner's session is absent or non-Open
+       orphan-mark + delete in batches of at most B                        # decision 5
+```
+
+Every step is one preconditioned, idempotent batch; a pass may stop anywhere and the next pass
+re-derives its work from durable records. The sweep is bounded per pass (`B` mutations per
+commit, decision 4) and never scans an unbounded namespace (admission, above).
+
+**Invariant preserved.** (3) globally — with the reaper deployed, every state in §2 has a
+driver; and (2) — every staged byte's exit is the reaper's when no client provides one.
+
+**Failure-mode table.**
+
+| Way to implement it wrong | Observable that fails |
+|---|---|
+| **False positive: reap a progressing upload.** Judge liveness by session age, or by "no part committed within W" alone | A single `UploadPart` streaming longer than `W_open` (DST: a slow body with lease renewals): the session MUST survive the reaper pass, and the part MUST commit afterwards. |
+| **The Complete race.** Reap a session concurrently with its Complete | DST both interleavings: (i) reaper fences first → Complete's fence returns `Conflict`, client sees `404 NoSuchUpload`, **no object is published**; (ii) Complete fences first → the reaper's CAS fails and it MUST NOT touch the session, and the published object's fragments MUST carry no `orphan:` record after the drain. |
+| **Roll back a `Completing` session unsafely** | DST: reaper rolls back while the completer's publication batch is in flight; assert the publication returns `Conflict` and that a subsequent Complete publishes exactly once. |
+| **Crash mid-teardown** | Kill the reaper at each drain step (seeded DST): re-running MUST converge to zero staged records with no fragment left unevidenced, and no orphan record re-stamped (grace clock preserved). |
+| **False negative: never reap** | A session abandoned for `> W_open` MUST be gone (records and bytes reclaimed) within a bounded number of passes; assert the staged population returns to zero. |
+| **The sweep breaks the bounded-work rule** | Reap a 10,000-part session and assert every commit is inside the envelope and that the pass makes durable partial progress when interrupted. |
+| **Arrival outruns drain** | Drive session creation faster than the reaper reclaims: creation MUST start refusing at `MAX_OPEN_SESSIONS` (`503`), and the staged record count MUST stay below the reference-set scan bound. Without admission, the custodian plane halts (`ScanCapExceeded`, `traits/src/lib.rs:275-292`). |
+| **Reaper unavailable** | Stop the reaper entirely, then drive creation: `reconcile_step` MUST keep succeeding indefinitely (no `ScanCapExceeded`), because admission refuses before the namespace can cross the cap. |
+| **Clock-epoch mismatch** | Write a session record with a foreign `clock_source`; the reaper MUST skip it and raise an operator signal, never reap it. |
+| **Reap by expiry of owned leases** | An `Open` session whose owned leases lapsed (a stalled but live client) MUST NOT have its bytes reclaimed until the session itself is abandoned and fenced. |
+
+---
+
+### Clock lifecycles this protocol introduces (F10, one table)
+
+`AGENTS.md:132-142` makes it a MUST that a new clock read states which source owns its
+lifecycle, and that one lifecycle never mixes sources.
+
+| Lifecycle | Stamp | Written by | Evaluated by | Owner |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Abandonment | `mpu.created_at_millis`, `part.committed_at_millis` | gateway | reaper | deployment wall clock (`SystemTime::now`, madsim-virtualised); guarded by `clock_source` — an unrecognized source is skipped, never reaped |
+| Orphan grace | `orphan:` value written by the retirement drain | whoever drains (gateway inline or reaper) | GC (`gc.rs:171-176`) | the same deployment wall clock, exactly as the overwrite path's `orphaned_at_millis` (`write.rs:305-313`) |
+| Staging lease | `pending.lease_expiry_millis` (owned entries) | the write path, renewed in flight (`write.rs:474-500`) | **nothing that decides reclamation** — owned entries are reclaimed by reference (decision 5); the lease decides only in-flight renewal | the write path |
+| Tombstone | `mpu.completed_at_millis` | gateway (publication batch) | reaper | deployment wall clock |
+
+No new clock is introduced, and the one lifecycle that could have mixed sources — reclamation
+of staged residue — is made clock-free instead.
+
+### Execution register — the enumeration, in one place
+
+The refutation test for this proposal is a concrete execution — a crash point, a lost CAS, a
+race, an operator action, a clock mismatch — that (a) strands bytes or metadata with no
+bounded reclamation path, (b) leaves a state nothing can exit, (c) publishes or preserves a
+chunk map over bytes a maintenance pass may reclaim or has reclaimed, or (d) installs an
+obligation past the transaction envelope. This table is the enumeration those outcomes are
+tested against; every row states what actually happens and what makes it so.
+
+| # | Execution | Outcome under this protocol | Disposed by |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| X1 | Concurrent `PutObject` wins the publication CAS (no crash) | Publish returns `Conflict`; bounded retry; else fence released to `Open`, `409` | D3 |
+| X2 | Completer crashes after fencing, before publishing | Session sits in `Completing`; reaper rolls it back to `Open` after `W_completing` | D3, D6 |
+| X3 | Completer crashes after the publication commit, before answering | Session is `Completed`; the client's retry answers `200` with the recorded ETag | D3 (F5) |
+| X4 | Publication returns `CommitUnknownResult` | Re-read the session record; it is the single authority on what happened | D3, `traits/src/lib.rs:730-745` |
+| X5 | Reaper fences an abandoned session while a Complete fences it | One CAS wins; loser gets `Conflict`. If the reaper won, nothing is published and the bytes are retired; if Complete won, the reaper does not touch the session | D6 |
+| X6 | Reaper rolls back a `Completing` session whose completer is merely slow | The slow publication fails its `require(mpu == Completing@E)` and publishes nothing; the client retries | D6 |
+| X7 | A part commit lands after a Complete fence | `require(mpu == Open@E)` fails; `404 NoSuchUpload`; the writer compensates its own chunks | D1, D5 |
+| X8 | Gateway crashes mid part-upload (fragments on disk, owned `pending:` entries) | Entries are owned by construction (intent precedes bytes); reclaimed by reference when the session leaves `Open` or vanishes | D5 |
+| X9 | Client re-uploads a part number that already has a record | The part commit replaces the record and installs `retire:bytes:{chunks}` for the superseded chunk list in the same batch | D4, D5 |
+| X10 | Two concurrent `UploadPart`s for the same part number | Exact-value precondition on the part key: one wins, the loser gets `Conflict` and compensates | D1, D5 |
+| X11 | Client aborts a 10,000-part session | One O(1) fence batch answers `204`; teardown drains asynchronously in `B`-sized batches | D3, D4 (F9) |
+| X12 | Drain crashes after orphan-marking, before deleting the part records | Bytes are both protected and evidenced; re-entry skips already-marked fragments, grace clock intact | D4, `restore.rs:92-100` |
+| X13 | Drain crashes after deleting records, before deleting the obligation | Bytes carry orphan evidence; GC reclaims after grace; the obligation is re-drained as a no-op | D4 |
+| X14 | Operator drains a D server holding staged fragments | `reconciliation_status` stays `Pending`; no new staged fragment lands there; clears within `W_open` | D2 |
+| X15 | Operator wipes that server anyway | The pre-wipe status was `Pending`, so the wipe is an operator override, not a silent `Satisfied` — the F6 trace is closed at the reporting step | D2 |
+| X16 | Metadata restored to a point before the session's part records existed | Those fragments are genuinely unreferenced (the session record is rewound too), so marking them is correct; a *surviving* session's staged fragments are protected by the staged reference set | D2, *Backward compatibility* |
+| X17 | 1-byte `PutObject` overwrites a max-size multipart object | Publication installs `retire:bytes:{generation}`; no inline fan-out; the commit fits the envelope | D4 (F4) |
+| X18 | Multipart Complete overwrites an existing multipart object | Same, plus `retire:records:` for its own staging records | D4 |
+| X19 | Abandoned sessions accumulate while the reaper is down | Admission refuses new sessions at the cap; the staged namespace never crosses `SCAN_CAP`; the custodian plane keeps reconciling | D6 (F7) |
+| X20 | Valid uploads arrive faster than the reaper drains | Same admission fence: `503 SlowDown`, backpressure to the client | D6 (F7) |
+| X21 | A session record carries a `clock_source` the reaper does not own | Skipped and alarmed — never reaped on a foreign stamp (the #557 class) | D6 (F10) |
+| X22 | Expired-pending sweep runs with `Reclaim` while a session is `Open` | Owned entries are skipped by rule; only the reference-based arm may reclaim them | D5 |
+| X23 | A D server holding staged fragments loses a disk | Scrub detects, reconstruction rebuilds and re-places under the session fence | D2 |
+| X24 | Reconstruction re-places a staged chunk while a Complete is fencing | The re-place CAS fails (`require(mpu == Open@E)`); the obligation stays queued and is served after publication | D2 |
+| X25 | Complete names only a subset of the staged parts | Named parts publish; unnamed parts' bytes are orphan-marked and their records deleted | D3, §1 |
+| X26 | The target bucket is deleted mid-session | The publication's bucket-existence precondition fails; `NoSuchBucket`; fence released | D3, [ADR-0046][a46] §4 |
+| X27 | The assembled map would exceed `MAX_MAP_CHUNKS` | Refused at `UploadPart` (early) and at Complete (authoritative); never an over-envelope commit | D4 |
+| X28 | Complete retried after the tombstone window | `404 NoSuchUpload`, with the object present and readable by its key | D3 (bounded, registered) |
+| X29 | An `orphan:` record exists for a chunk that is committed-referenced | GC's reference gate is evaluated **first** (`gc.rs:159-176`), so the record is inert while the reference stands | GC precedence, defence in depth |
+| X30 | Two sessions Complete on the same key concurrently | Both publish through the inode CAS; one supersedes the other; the superseded generation is retired | D3, D4 |
+| X31 | Gateway restarts (fresh random chunk epoch) with sessions open | Chunk ids never collide across epochs and are `>= 2^127`, clear of allocator recovery; sessions are unaffected — a second gateway can complete them | `crates/server/src/lib.rs:237-251` |
+| X32 | A part commit returns `CommitUnknownResult` and the batch later lands | The writer re-reads before compensating; only a settled not-committed outcome triggers compensation, so a live part's chunks are never orphan-marked | D5, `traits/src/lib.rs:730-745` |
+| X33 | A backend rejects a batch for size | Only reachable through a value bounded by `MAX_MAP_CHUNKS` / `MAX_PART_CHUNKS`, which admission refuses past — see the batch inventory for the per-batch bound | D4 |
+
