+## Alternatives considered
+
+- **Keep lease liveness and renew the leases of staged parts.** Rejected: it puts a timer on an
+  inherently long-lived operation, and it makes the *renewal loop* a correctness dependency for
+  data whose client may be offline for hours. It also leaves the staged bytes inside
+  `pending:`, where the deployed `Defer` posture makes their residue unreclaimable (F3).
+- **A per-session mutation counter, CAS'd by every part commit.** Would give Complete a
+  one-precondition proof exactly as the fence does, but it serializes every `UploadPart` of one
+  upload through a single hot key — S3 clients upload 4–16 parts concurrently by default, so
+  this converts parallel uploads into a retry storm. The fence buys the same proof with a CAS
+  only on *state transitions*, which are rare.
+- **Per-part exact-value preconditions at Complete.** Correct, and it is the shape a first
+  implementation reaches for; rejected on the envelope: 10,000 part values in one batch cannot
+  fit inside 10 MB / 5 s (`traits/src/lib.rs:744-758`), so the largest legal upload would be
+  the one that cannot complete.
+- **Staging inside `pending:` with a longer TTL.** Rejected: it is exactly the "synthesized
+  encoding into an existing namespace" [ADR-0046][a46] rejected for buckets, it inherits the
+  expiry-based reclamation hazard (#557), and it makes every consumer of `pending:` — GC,
+  restore, the sweep, allocator recovery — implicitly a consumer of multipart semantics.
+- **Inline orphan expansion with a "large object" special case.** Two code paths, two
+  correctness stories, and the boundary between them is exactly where the bug hides. The
+  retirement ledger is one path for both.
+- **Synchronous Abort teardown.** Rejected on the same envelope arithmetic as above, and it
+  would make Abort's latency proportional to the session's size — an unbounded HTTP response
+  time for the verb clients call *to recover*.
+- **A smaller reference-set scan bound.** Rejected: choosing a smaller cap only makes the halt
+  arrive earlier (`SCAN_CAP` is a correctness constraint, not a tuning knob,
+  `traits/src/lib.rs:275-292`). The bound has to come from admission.
+- **Chunk-map segmentation now** (splitting a published map across several records). This is
+  the *general* answer to the value ceiling and would lift the object-size bound to S3's 5 TiB.
+  Rejected for this proposal: it changes the shape of `InodeRecord` for every consumer — the
+  read path, GC's reference build, reconstruction's `find_chunk`, rebalance, backfill, and
+  every backend's conformance expectations — which is an ADR-scale change to the object model
+  ([ADR-0047][a47]'s successor), not a multipart decision. It is registered as a follow-up and
+  as the accepted cost's exit.
+- **Letting staged bytes go unscrubbed and unrepaired** (the smaller diff for decision 2).
+  Rejected because the cost class is durability, not availability: over a staging window
+  measured in hours, unrepaired fragment loss accumulates and Complete would publish a map over
+  fewer than `k` survivors. Only availability, latency, capacity and operational costs are
+  acceptable trade-offs here.
+
+## Graduation criteria
+
+### The F1–F10 disposition list
+
+Exactly one disposition per row: **eliminated** by the design (with the observable that would
+catch a regression), a **bounded non-safety cost** (with bound, rationale, and follow-up), or
+**flagged NEEDS-HUMAN** (never self-accepted). No refutation-standard outcome — (a) stranded
+with no bounded reclamation, (b) a state nothing can exit, (c) a map over reclaimable bytes,
+(d) an obligation past the envelope — is disposed of as an accepted cost.
+
+| # | Disposition | Mechanism | Binding observable |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **F1** absorbing state on publication CAS loss | **Eliminated** | Decision 3: bounded retry, then fence release to `Open`; reaper rollback for a crashed completer; §2's exit table | The F1 trace as a test: after a concurrent `PutObject` wins, the session is never left in `Completing`; and a state-machine test asserting every state has a taken exit |
+| **F2** staging-record disposal at publication | **Eliminated** | `retire:records:` installed in the publication batch; drained in bounded batches; the mode lives in the key | After Complete + full drain: `scan("part:<id>:")` is empty, and **no** `orphan:` record exists for any published chunk |
+| **F3** abort-race residue unreclaimable under `Defer` | **Eliminated** | Decision 5: ownership tag, loser compensation, reference-based backstop; expiry sweep skips owned entries | Kill a part upload, run the deployed configuration (`Defer`, no attestation): residue reclaimed within `W_open`; and owned entries survive an expiry sweep while their session is `Open` |
+| **F4** obligation fan-out vs. the envelope | **Eliminated** for the fan-out; **bounded capacity cost** for the map ceiling | Decision 4: retirement ledger for every superseding publication; admission bound on map size, enforced by refusal | Overwrite a max-size multipart object with a 1-byte PUT on FoundationDB: it commits, and every commit in the batch inventory stays inside the envelope. **Cost:** max object size is `MAX_MAP_CHUNKS × chunk_size`, well under S3's 5 TiB. **Follow-up:** FU-1 (chunk-map segmentation) |
+| **F5** non-idempotent Complete / retry after unknown outcome | **Eliminated** | Publication and the `Completed` transition are one batch; the session record is the durable evidence; re-read protocol per `traits/src/lib.rs:730-745`; tombstone window | Retry Complete after an injected `CommitUnknownResult`: exactly one publication (`version` advances once), one obligation set, identical ETag returned |
+| **F6** maintenance-pass visibility split | **Eliminated** (safety half); **bounded availability cost** (drain half) | Decision 2's per-consumer table; staged placement excludes draining servers | The wipe trace: drain a server holding staged fragments → `Pending`, never `Satisfied`. **Cost:** a drain waits up to `W_open` for staged uploads. **Follow-up:** FU-2 (operator-forced staged evacuation) |
+| **F7** unbounded staged state halts the custodian plane | **Eliminated** | Admission control (the cardinality bound that holds *without* the reaper) **plus** the reaper (progress) | Reaper stopped + sustained creation: `reconcile_step` keeps succeeding and creation refuses with `503`; assert staged records never approach `SCAN_CAP` |
+| **F8** vacuous or unsound reaper design | **Eliminated** | Decision 6: liveness derived from records the protocol writes (part stamps + owned leases), clock-sound detection, the full false-positive / false-negative / race / crash table, and the normative `#625` ordering | Decision 6's failure-mode table in full, run as seeded DST |
+| **F9** client-visible semantics of fenced states | **Eliminated** | Decision 3's verb × state table; Abort answers from the fence commit alone | A wire-level test per cell of the table (owned by #508), plus: Abort of a 10,000-part session answers from one O(1) batch |
+| **F10** clock-lifecycle ownership | **Eliminated** | The clock-lifecycle table: one lifecycle, one source, `clock_source` guard, and staged reclamation made clock-free | A foreign `clock_source` session is skipped and alarmed, never reaped; DST with skewed producers reclaims nothing it should not |
+
+### Accepted costs register
+
+Each is a bounded availability / latency / capacity / operational trade-off. None is a
+refutation outcome.
+
+| Cost | Bound | Rationale | Follow-up |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Max object size is `MAX_MAP_CHUNKS × chunk_size`, below S3's 5 TiB | the bound is a stated constant, refused at `UploadPart` and at Complete | the value ceiling is inherited and pre-existing (a 5 GiB single PUT already crosses it); refusing is safe, committing is not | **FU-1** — chunk-map segmentation (raises the ceiling; ADR-scale, successor to ADR-0047's record shape) |
+| A drain waits for staged uploads on the draining server | `W_open` | the alternative is the F6 wipe trace, a safety outcome | **FU-2** — an operator-forced staged evacuation (fence sessions holding fragments on a decommissioning server) |
+| Prior-generation bytes are retained slightly longer than today (grace starts at drain, not at supersede) | one reaper cadence plus the grace window | evidence must precede the lifting of protection; the direction is strictly safe | **FU-3** — drain-rate telemetry and an alert on obligation age |
+| A retried Complete after `W_tombstone` answers `404` although the object exists | `W_tombstone` | unbounded tombstones are an unbounded namespace | none required (S3-conforming) |
+| A reap of a *progressing but silent* upload costs the client its staged parts | `W_open`, operator-chosen inside the settled range | reaping is safe (the fence prevents any partial publication); the cost is re-upload | **FU-4** — surface the abandonment window in the S3 error text |
