+
+### ADR graduation recommendation
+
+Two of these decisions outlive multipart and should graduate to ADRs after this proposal is
+accepted (authoring them is follow-up work under [ADR-0037][a37], not part of this document):
+
+1. **The retirement ledger — durable obligations installed atomically, drained in bounded,
+   idempotent batches** (decision 4). It applies to every publication that supersedes a prior
+   generation, to server-side copy (#504 step 2), and to any future resumable write. It also
+   changes an existing committed contract (`commit_chunk_map_superseding`), which is precisely
+   the class of decision an ADR records.
+2. **The staging protection class and per-consumer visibility rule** (decision 2). It changes
+   what the maintenance plane's reference set *means* — a contract every custodian pass and
+   every future maintenance pass inherits.
+
+Decisions 1, 3, 5 and 6 are multipart-shaped enough to live in this proposal; if server-side
+copy adopts decision 1's fence verbatim, that is the moment to reconsider.
+
+## Backward compatibility
+
+- **On-disk / record format.** Purely additive: three new key prefixes, and one optional field
+  on `PendingEntry` that is `#[serde(default)]` **and** `skip_serializing_if =
+  "Option::is_none"`, so a record written before the change decodes unchanged and re-encodes
+  byte-identically — the CAS-identity rule [ADR-0047][a47] established for `InodeRecord`. No
+  existing record's meaning changes. `InodeRecord` itself is untouched: an object published by
+  multipart is an ordinary object.
+- **Version skew during rollout — a hard ordering requirement.** A custodian build **without**
+  decision 2's staged awareness, run against a store where a newer gateway is staging parts, is
+  dangerous in one specific way: the restore pass marks every unreferenced, non-pending fragment
+  `orphan:` (`restore.rs:257-269`), so it would mark live staged fragments and GC would reclaim
+  them after the grace window. GC itself is safe (its conservative arm keeps fragments with no
+  orphan record, `gc.rs:183-187`), and so is scrub (it walks only what the reference set names).
+  Therefore: **the custodian plane, including the restore tool, MUST be upgraded before any
+  gateway is allowed to create multipart sessions.** The implementing slices should make this
+  checkable rather than documentary (a refusal or an alarm when the staging namespace is
+  non-empty and staged awareness is absent).
+- **Deployments.** No new external dependency, no new service, no new port. The reaper is
+  another pass in the existing custodian loop, dispatched from the same fenced control point
+  (`reconciliation.rs:75-112`).
+- **Public API.** Additive: the multipart verbs (#508). Existing PUT/GET/DELETE semantics are
+  unchanged, with one deliberate internal change — a superseding publication installs a
+  retirement obligation instead of expanding orphan records inline (decision 4). The
+  operator-visible consequence is that reclamation of a superseded generation is asynchronous;
+  the reader-safe grace guarantee is unchanged, and reclamation is never *earlier* than today.
+- **Rollback.** Disabling the multipart verbs after sessions exist leaves records the reaper
+  still drains; the reaper must therefore keep running (and keep its rows compiled in) even when
+  the verbs are disabled. Removing the reaper while sessions exist is the one unsupported
+  downgrade — it re-creates F1 and F7 by construction.
+
+## Open questions
+
+Each of these is explicitly **non-gating for #508** and carries its reason and its owner. None
+of the six decisions and none of F1–F10 is parked here.
+
+- **Multipart ETag composition.** [ADR-0047][a47] closed the ETag *basis* (lowercase-hex
+  SHA-256 as an opaque change-token; MD5 rejected) and deferred only the multipart
+  *composition* to the implementing slice. Non-gating for the commit protocol because the
+  protocol needs exactly one property from it — the published ETag must be a **pure function of
+  the part records' recorded digests and their order**, so a re-derived value on a retried
+  Complete is identical, and the value is in any case recorded in the `Completed` session
+  record. Owner: **#508**.
+- **Knob values.** Every correctness-relevant knob has its range and bounding invariant settled
+  above; choosing the values inside those ranges, and wiring the metrics, alerts and CLI flags,
+  is non-gating because no value inside a settled range can break an invariant. Owners:
+  **#625** (`B`, `W_open`, `W_completing`, `W_tombstone`, cadence), **#508**
+  (`MAX_MAP_CHUNKS`, `MAX_PART_CHUNKS`, `chunk_size`, `MAX_OPEN_SESSIONS`, `R_publish`).
+- **Chunk-map segmentation (FU-1).** Non-gating because #508 ships with the admission bound and
+  refuses rather than commits past it; segmentation only *raises* the ceiling. Owner: a
+  follow-up ADR + slice, to be filed on acceptance of this proposal.
+- **Operator-forced staged evacuation (FU-2).** Non-gating because the drain completes within
+  `W_open` without it. Owner: follow-up, alongside the management surface (proposal 0008).
+- **Whether `ExpiredPendingPolicy::Defer` can be retired once every producer stamps live
+  leases** (#490 / #557). Untouched by this proposal — staged reclamation is clock-free
+  precisely so it does not depend on that question being answered. Owner: **#557**.
+- **Per-bucket lifecycle expiry of incomplete uploads** (S3's `AbortIncompleteMultipartUpload`).
+  Non-gating: the reaper's `W_open` already bounds the namespace; a per-bucket policy is a
